\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Changes of source and target inner-product coordinates}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For an \vocabk{ordered Gram pair}{fdip-018P}, let
#{u:E\to E'} and #{v_j:F_j\to F'_j} be unitary. The couplings
#{Y'_j=uY_jv_j^{-1}} have products
##{Y'_j(Y'_j)^\dagger=u(Y_jY_j^\dagger)u^{-1}\qquad(j=1,2).}
The products are positive semidefinite Hermitian operators. The
trace of each word in them is unchanged. Every ordered pair of
positive semidefinite operators on #{E} occurs in this way as
the two products of maps into #{E}.}

\proof{
\p{The adjoint of the transformed map is
#{v_jY_j^\dagger u^{-1}}. The two neighboring source unitaries
cancel in each product. In a word the common target unitaries
likewise cancel at every internal position, leaving one common
conjugation, which preserves trace by cyclicity as in
\ref{fdip-00KF}. Each product is self-adjoint and its quadratic
form at #{x} is #{\|Y_j^\dagger x\|^2}. For the converse, a Hermitian
operator in positive complex dimension has an eigenvector by its characteristic polynomial.
Its eigenvalue is real by self-adjointness. The orthogonal
complement of that eigenvector is invariant, so induction gives
an orthonormal eigenbasis. Positivity gives #{\lambda\ge0}
on each eigenspace. Acting by
#{\sqrt{\lambda}} there gives a self-adjoint map with the
desired product, including zero on its kernel. Do this
independently for the two operators.}
}
